83
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
oh, I've got a couple of brief email questions to answer,

84
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
But in Scout Mastership, in seven minutes or less,

85
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:37,000
something that will phrase as youth engagement in scouting

86
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
and explain exactly what I mean by that.

87
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
So, hey, let's get started, shall we

88
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Scout Mastership in seven minutes

89
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Or less?

90
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
So, when we're really hitting on all eight cylinders,

91
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
scouting is something that Scouts do for themselves

92
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
rather than just like this program of activities

93
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:05,000
that is presented to them.

94
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
There's a big difference there, right?

95
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:11,000
If we are just putting together a series of programs

96
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:14,000
and activities that Scouts participate in,

97
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:18,000
they're not really fully engaged in what's going on

98
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
and we're not really tapping the potential

99
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
and the opportunities that scouting presents to us,

100
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Because the context of scouting means our youth members

101
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
are engaged in leading one another

102
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
and in making decisions affecting what they do

103
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,000
and how it's done.

104
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:37,000
I want to call this youth engagement

105
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
rather than youth leadership-

106
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
And I'm being pretty careful about the terminology here.

107
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
I'm saying youth engagement,

108
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
I'm not saying youth leadership,

109
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
or boiling it down to that famous two-word phrase-

110
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:51,000
boy led-

111
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
And the reason that I think we need to differentiate,

112
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
because a lot of times we look at youth leadership

113
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
as kind of like a light switch:

114
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,000
It's on or it's off.

115
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
We're boy led or we're not boy led.

116
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Now what we need to do is to engage all of our Scouts

117
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
in a partnership where they're leading one another

118
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:13,000
and they're making decisions affecting what they do

119
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:14,000
and how it's done.

120
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,000
Our ideal role as Scouts is being a barely perceptible influence

121
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:22,000
that empowers young people to do things for themselves,

122
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:27,000
And we accomplish this through a kind of a progressive partnership

123
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
of adult and youth roles that are collaborative

124
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:33,000
and not necessarily hierarchical.

125
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Now, there's a couple of great $9 college words right there.

126
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
So what I'm saying is is that we are facilitators,

127
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
enablers, helpers, mentors, guides, coaches.

128
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
We're not issuing orders.

129
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,000
We're working with our Scouts in a way that they are fully engaged

130
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:51,000
in what's going on.

131
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:55,000
And this partnership, this kind of collaborative partnership,

132
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
is one of the unique facets of Scouting

133
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
that adults generally misunderstand.

134
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
We can be very reluctant to put trust and authority

135
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
in young people or doubt the ability of young people

136
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,000
to exercise that trust or that authority responsibly.

137
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
But engaging young people in these things

138
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
does not mean just purposeless anarchy.

139
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
I mean Scouting has boundaries. in the same way

140
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:22,000
the lines chalked on a playing field

141
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
describe the boundaries of a game.

142
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Both Scouts and Scouters observe the boundaries

143
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,000
and we have a shared commitment

144
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
that's based on mutual trust and patience.

145
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
And that's why, as I said before,

146
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
I want to be really careful about the terminology

147
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,000
and call it youth engagement rather than youth leadership,

148
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:46,000
because it's not this kind of binary, on-off thing,

149
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:47,000
It's progressive.

150
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:49,000
We could do a much better job

151
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
of building that progression in early on,

152
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
when our young people are in Cub Scouts

153
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:57,000
And the opportunities are there

154
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,000
but they're not necessarily connected

155
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:01,000
with what happens in the future.

156
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
We want the youngest youth we're working with,

157
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:07,000
helping with simpler tasks and some decision-making

158
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
and peer-to-peer work and sharing

159
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,000
and helping each other right,

160
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:14,000
And we build on that by giving them

161
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:16,000
more directive leadership roles

162
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:19,000
and intermediate tasks as they get a little bit older,

163
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,000
more influence on choosing activities

164
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
and decision-making and instructing

165
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
and leading each other,

166
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:27,000
And then we build that towards more independent

167
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:29,000
decision-making and leadership roles.

168
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
This progression, if it hasn't started early on

169
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
or if it's just simply not present

170
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
with the youth that we're working with.

171
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:40,000
it's going to take some time for this to all catch,

172
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
for everybody to understand their roles

173
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:44,000
and things to get going.

174
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,000
And there's a real simple kind of three-step formula

175
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:50,000
for this by Greenbar Bill William Hillcourt

176
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:52,000
in an earlier Scoutmasters' Handbook.

177
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
Very simple idea:

178
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,000
Train them, trust them and let them lead.

179
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
So let's pick that apart for a moment.

180
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:00,000
Training,

181
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,000
So we wouldn't begin instructing Scouts

182
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
learning how to tie a square knot, for instance,

183
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:08,000
with long theoretical discourse on knots and rope.

184
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:11,000
We put a rope in their hand and we let them try.

185
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:14,000
So if you're going to engage youth in leadership,

186
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
the most effective way to develop those skills for them

187
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
is by getting them to lead,

188
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
And that's engaging them in actual

189
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
rather than theoretical leadership,

190
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:28,000
because leadership skills develop as you lead,

191
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:32,000
not in a training session or in a theoretical environment.

192
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
And we have a way as adults a lot of times

193
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
of over-explaining and over-instructing

194
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
in a way that our Scouts find very frustrating.

195
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
So let's not overload them with theoretical information.

196
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:46,000
Give them the basics and let them at it.

197
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
Follow up with reflection and mentoring

198
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:52,000
to steer developing leaders towards more confidence,

199
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
more success and more understanding.

200
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:56,000
Training our youth members to be engaged

201
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,000
with what they're doing is not an event.

202
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
It's not something that we can sit down and do

203
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
with PowerPoint slides or in a theoretical environment.

204
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:06,000
They have to actually start doing things

205
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
and then, once they've done them

206
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:10,000
and they've had a little experience, reflecting on them

207
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
for a very short period of time

208
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
before they move on to the next challenge

209
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
is a really great idea, because then

210
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:19,000
they start to understand the complexities

211
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
of what they are doing.

212
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:24,000
So the next thing Green Bar Bill says is

213
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
trust them, says train them, then trust them

214
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:29,000
and think about this. okay,

215
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:32,000
Maybe you haven't had the pleasure yet,

216
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
but if you have a child, at one point

217
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
you're probably going to get involved

218
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
with helping them learn how to drive

219
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:39,000
and get a driver's license.

220
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:41,000
And if you're going to ride along in the car

221
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:42,000
with them as they're learning,

222
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,000
you know what I'm talking about.

223
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
It's very challenging

224
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
to put a whole lot of trust

225
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
in what they're doing,

226
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
and letting scouts engage in leadership

227
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:54,000
feels a lot like sitting in the passenger seat

228
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
while a student driver steers the car.

229
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Now we may understand how important it is

230
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
to let go, but letting go is never easy.

231
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:05,000
Trusting youth leaders is always a challenge,

232
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,000
but it gets easier as you see

233
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
what they are capable of achieving.

234
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
And next is: let them

235
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:13,000
Let them lead.

236
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:16,000
So we can't give scouts the responsibility

237
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:19,000
and then undermine it with continual interference.

238
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
And that interference can come in many guises.

239
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
It can come as wanting to help,

240
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
or wanting to instruct or train,

241
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
or just being a little anxious

242
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:31,000
about letting them carry out

243
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
a given activity.

244
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
So it's real responsibility

245
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,000
and letting them lead

246
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
and letting them learn

247
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:39,000
as they move along.

248
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:42,000
So let's talk about real responsibility,

249
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
and real responsibility

250
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
means that we haven't eliminated

251
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
all the possibilities of failing

252
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:49,000
in the situation.

253
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
I mean, we want our scouts to succeed, naturally,

254
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
but this doesn't mean we absolutely

255
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
protect them from failing.

256
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
We ought to expect and learn to welcome

257
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
all the little bumps and setbacks

258
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
and failures that come along the way,

259
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
because they're a vital tool

260
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:05,000
for developing skill.

261
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:06,000
If you think about it,

262
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
any skill that you claim right now

263
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
you develop by making mistakes,

264
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
And the wonderful thing about scouting

265
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:15,000
is that we put our young people

266
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
in a safe environment

267
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
so that they can make plenty of mistakes

268
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
and thereby learn and develop some skill.

269
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
Any championship sports team

270
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
has usually suffered at least

271
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
one or two losses somewhere along the way, right,

272
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
And the difference between a championship season

273
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
and a losing season

274
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
kind of hinges on learning the lessons

275
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
from those losses or those failures.

276
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
Handled the right way,

277
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
All of the things that go wrong

278
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
can be a source of intense motivation

279
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
for athletes and for scouts.

280
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:49,000
We need to respond to mistakes and failures

281
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
with good humor and encouragement

282
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
and commiseration, because we've all been there.

283
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
We need to understand

284
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
that failures and missteps are inevitable

285
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
and they're valuable

286
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
and they're opportunities to grow.

287
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
They're not crimes to prosecute and punish

288
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
And we can encourage leadership, development

289
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
and accountability

290
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
without being real dramatic

291
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:13,000
or having to deal with punishments or bad feelings.

292
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
And the right attitude on our part

293
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
will inspire the initiative in our young people.

294
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:21,000
And that attitude can be a real challenge

295
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
as an adult, because a lot of us have tried

296
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
at least once or twice or three times

297
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
to engage scouts in meaningful leadership

298
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
and if they don't respond as we'd hoped,

299
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
we get impatient and discouraged

300
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:36,000
and we tend to reserve important decisions

301
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
and authority to ourselves

302
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
because we know that we tried that a couple of times

303
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
and it doesn't work.

304
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
Look, scouts are no fools.

305
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:48,000
They will wait until you do everything for them,

306
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
because in a lot of ways

307
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
that's normally what happens

308
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
in just about every area of their lives.

309
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
But we want to inspire them

310
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
to take the initiative of making things happen,

311
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
and we have to understand

312
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
that what might seem routine to us

313
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
may be totally new

314
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
to one of the young people that we're working with,

315
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
and I think it's unfair to expect

316
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:11,000
an inexperienced scout to respond

317
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:14,000
the same way an experienced adult would

318
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
when they're faced with a leadership challenge.

319
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
And scouts always show initiative

320
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
if we learn to look for it.

321
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
Sometimes it's very small,

322
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
but when anybody is undertaking a new enterprise,

323
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
like engaging themselves in leadership,

324
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
they are vulnerable.

325
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
They need reassurance to get past

326
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
their problems.

327
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
So instead of looking for what's wrong,

328
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
we do a lot of work looking for what's right.

329
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
We look for the least sign of initiative

330
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
and we support it and we build on it,

331
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
no matter how small it might be.

332
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
So we want to watch how we react

333
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
to what scouts do

334
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:50,000
and we want to find that kind of advisory role

335
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
and that advisory voice.

336
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:55,000
And I'm not talking about tone or volume,

337
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
but the motivation behind what you say.

338
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
If you're really

339
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
invested in engaging youth in leadership,

340
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:05,000
you will help articulate

341
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
the concerns of all the scouts,

342
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
to motivate your youth leaders to service.

343
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
So let me explain what I mean by that.

344
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
Why are we engaging youth in leadership?

345
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
Is it to satisfy the demands

346
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
that we're making on them? No,

347
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
They aren't serving us,

348
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
they're serving their fellow scouts.

349
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:26,000
And what do their fellow scouts need from them?

350
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
They want to go camping

351
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
in advance and do scouting with their friends. right,

352
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
An advisor is keeping that

353
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
in front of the youth leadership all the time.

354
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
Hey, you're serving these scouts.

355
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
what do these scouts want?

356
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
Are you making it happen for them?

357
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
You're not serving me. I'm not your boss,

358
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
I'm not your drill sergeant.

359
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
You're serving your fellow scouts

360
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
and I'm just here to help you make that happen.

361
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
So this whole concept of engaging youth

362
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
and the way that the opportunities we present

363
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
for them to develop leadership skills

364
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
needs to be a progressive thing.

365
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
and then the work that we do

366
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
with them to advise, to instruct,

367
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,000
to mentor, to coach, to guide-

368
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:09,000
are things that are simple to articulate in a podcast,

369
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:12,000
but they're very complex things to learn

370
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and it takes time.

371
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
So you have to be patient with yourself too,

372
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
because in scouting, the relationship

373
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
of adults to youth leaders

374
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
is something that you will not find

375
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
very often in the other endeavors

376
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,000
that they are undertaking during this time of their lives.

377
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
So, as you go out and you work with your scouts.

378
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
think about youth engagement

379
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:37,000
as a way of helping them develop leadership skills.

380
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:58,000
E-mail, that is folks.

381
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:01,000
And here's an answer to one of your e-mails.

382
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Sean Earp is the Scoutmaster, Troop 330

383
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
in Auburn, Washington,

384
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
and he wrote with this question. he says:

385
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
sometimes, as a new-ish Scoutmaster,

386
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
I'm finding it challenging to figure out

387
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,000
why our troop does things the way that it does.

388
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
Is it because the previous Scoutmaster

389
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
preferred to run things that way?

390
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:47,000
Is it a question of policy?

391
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
Is it a

392
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:52,000
Or is it a question of

393
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
policy?

394
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:57,000
Or is it some kind of inviolable precept

395
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
handed down by Baden Powell?

396
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
For example, when we go camping,

397
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
our patrol is set up and camp and cook

398
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
as a patrol, separate from the adults.

399
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
But I'm trying to figure out

400
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
where do the senior patrol leader

401
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
and assistant senior patrol leader

402
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
set up their camp and eat?

403
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
As long as I've been with our troop,

404
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
the senior patrol leader and assistant senior patrol leader

405
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
have been required to set up their tents

406
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
and eat with the adults.

407
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,000
So I wanted to see if that's the norm.

408
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
I see no guidance in any of the literature,

409
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
not in the troop leader guidebook

410
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
or the Boy Scout handbook

411
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
or the senior patrol leader handbook.

412
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
Sean, that's an interesting question.

413
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
I'll tell you: my practice has been

414
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
to have those youth leaders

415
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
who are not in a patrol camp

416
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
and cook with the adults too.

417
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
Now, we don't require them to

418
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
by any means,

419
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
It's just an expectation that has been created

420
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
by long practice.

421
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
I know that it should be,

422
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
as really never come up recently,

423
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
but that would probably be a really good thing

424
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
for you to do with your youth leaders

425
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
is to sit down and ask them

426
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
how they see the issue

427
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
and if they want to change the way things are done.

428
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
They may come up with an idea

429
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
that neither of us have thought of.

430
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
Daryl Oakley is the scout master,

431
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
Boy Scout troop 132 and up in Massachusetts,

432
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
and he wrote with this question.

433
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
he says: I've been through youth protection

434
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
a couple of times.

435
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
I'm one contact role.

436
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
I understand that when I email, text

437
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
or otherwise contact a scout,

438
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
there's always a parent

439
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
or a scouter included in the message or conversation.

440
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
I know when I have a scout master

441
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
conference, I do it in plain sight of others.

442
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
but what happens when I pick up

443
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
the phone and call a scout?

444
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
Do I have to have like another party

445
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
on the line with the scout in myself

446
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
If a scout asked me for a merit badge counselor

447
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
and he calls the merit badge counselor

448
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
should his parents be listening

449
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
to the phone upstairs.

450
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
This is all really kind of confusing.

451
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
Well, Daryl, yes and no,

452
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
I mean, you have it right.

453
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
There's no one-on-one contact.

454
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
Just keep that in mind and keep it simple,

455
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
and it's really not all that confusing

456
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
when you think about it. You're on the phone

457
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
with a scout. think about it

458
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
in the same way that you would think

459
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
about talking to a scout at a meeting

460
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
or during a scout master's conference.

461
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
We don't go into the other room and close the door.

462
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
No, we do it out in the open

463
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
or we keep the door to the room open or whatever.

464
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
So you pick up the phone and call a scout

465
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
and his parents pick up the line.

466
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
Hey, I wanted to talk to your son

467
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
for a moment about scouts. Would that be okay?

468
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
Are you making a one-on-one contact there?

469
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
Well, you're talking one-on-one with the scout,

470
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
but somebody else is aware that this is going on.

471
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
So you call on the phone

472
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
and the scout picks it up.

473
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
You say, hey, I wanted to talk to you

474
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
about a couple of scout things.

475
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
Is your mom or dad home? Could you put them on the line

476
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
for a moment, Or you are

477
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
someone else's presence when you're making the phone call

478
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
and you say, hey look, I'm going to call John.

479
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
He's our senior patrol leader.

480
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
You don't have to listen in,

481
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
but just follow my side of the conversation

482
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
for a couple of minutes, if you would.

483
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
I hope that helps clear the confusion up.

484
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
but that's usually the way that I observe

485
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
the youth protection thing. when it comes to telephone calls,

486
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
I make sure that there's a third party involved

487
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
somewhere and that they know

488
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
that that conversation is happening.

489
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
Hey, if you have a comment or question,

